.TH GREP 1 "May 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
grep, egrep, fgrep \- print lines matching a pattern
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grep
.RI [ OPTION ...]
.I PATTERN
.RI [ FILE ...]
.br
.B grep
.RI [ OPTION ...]
.BR \-e " " \fIPATTERN\fR " ... "
.RI [ FILE ...]
.br
.B grep
.RI [ OPTION ...]
.BR \-f " " \fIPATTERN_FILE\fR " ... "
.RI [ FILE ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B grep
searches the named input
.IR FILE s
for lines containing a match to the given
.IR PATTERN .
If no
.I FILE
is given, or when
.I FILE
is
.BR \- ,
.B grep
reads standard input.
By default,
.B grep
prints the matching lines.
.PP
Two related programs are provided as symbolic links to
.BR grep ,
and select a pattern dialect by their name:
.BR egrep
behaves as
.B grep \-E
(extended regular expressions) and
.B fgrep
behaves as
.B grep \-F
(fixed strings).
Both are deprecated by POSIX but retained for compatibility.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Pattern selection and interpretation
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-extended\-regexp
Interpret
.I PATTERN
as a POSIX extended regular expression (ERE).
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-fixed\-strings
Interpret
.I PATTERN
as a set of fixed strings, separated by newlines, any of which is to be
matched.
.TP
.BR \-G ", " \-\-basic\-regexp
Interpret
.I PATTERN
as a POSIX basic regular expression (BRE).  This is the default.
.TP
.BR \-e " " \fIPATTERN\fR ", " \-\-regexp= \fIPATTERN\fR
Use
.I PATTERN
as the pattern.  May be repeated; a line matches if any pattern matches.
.TP
.BR \-f " " \fIFILE\fR ", " \-\-file= \fIFILE\fR
Obtain patterns from
.IR FILE ,
one per line.  An empty file contains no patterns and matches nothing.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-case
Ignore case distinctions in both the pattern and the input.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-word\-regexp
Select only lines containing matches that form whole words.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-line\-regexp
Select only matches that exactly cover a whole line.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-invert\-match
Select non-matching lines.
.SS Bracket expressions
.B grep
supports POSIX bracket character classes
.RB ( [:alpha:] ", " [:digit:] ", " [:alnum:] ", " [:upper:] ", " [:lower:] ,
.BR [:space:] ", " [:blank:] ", " [:punct:] ", " [:cntrl:] ", " [:graph:] ,
.BR [:print:] ", " [:xdigit:] )
inside bracket expressions, evaluated as ASCII.
Collating symbols
.RB ( [[.ch.]] )
and equivalence classes
.RB ( [[=a=]] )
are not supported.
.SS General output control
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-count
Suppress normal output; instead print a count of selected lines per file.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-files\-with\-matches
Suppress normal output; instead print the name of each input file from which
output would normally have been printed.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-files\-without\-match
Print the name of each input file from which no output would normally have
been printed.
.TP
.BR \-m " " \fINUM\fR ", " \-\-max\-count= \fINUM\fR
Stop reading a file after
.I NUM
selected lines.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-only\-matching
Print only the matched (non-empty) parts of a matching line, with each such
part on a separate output line.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet ", " \-\-silent
Quiet; write nothing to standard output.  Exit immediately with zero status
if any match is found.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-no\-messages
Suppress error messages about nonexistent or unreadable files.
.SS Output line prefix control
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-byte\-offset
Print the 0-based byte offset within the input file before each output line
(or, with
.BR \-o ,
before each matched part).
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-with\-filename
Print the file name for each match.  This is the default with more than one
file.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-no\-filename
Suppress the prefixing of file names on output.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-line\-number
Prefix each line of output with its 1-based line number within its file.
.TP
.BI \-\-label= LABEL
Display input actually coming from standard input as input coming from file
.IR LABEL .
.SS Context line control
.TP
.BR \-A " " \fINUM\fR ", " \-\-after\-context= \fINUM\fR
Print
.I NUM
lines of trailing context after matching lines.
.TP
.BR \-B " " \fINUM\fR ", " \-\-before\-context= \fINUM\fR
Print
.I NUM
lines of leading context before matching lines.
.TP
.BR \-C " " \fINUM\fR ", " \-\-context= \fINUM\fR ", " \-\fINUM\fR
Print
.I NUM
lines of output context.  Groups separated by intervening non-context lines
are delimited by a line containing
.BR \-\- .
.SS File and directory selection
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-R ", " \-\-recursive
Read all files under each directory, recursively.  With no
.I FILE
operand, search the working directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-include= GLOB
During recursive search, examine only files whose base name matches
.IR GLOB .
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude= GLOB
During recursive search, skip files whose base name matches
.IR GLOB .
.TP
.BR \-d " " \fIACTION\fR ", " \-\-directories= \fIACTION\fR
If an input file is a directory, use
.IR ACTION " (" read ", " skip ", or " recurse )
to process it.  The default is
.BR skip .
.SS Binary file handling
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-text
Process a binary file as if it were text.
.TP
.B \-I
Process a binary file as if it contained no matching data.
.TP
.BI \-\-binary\-files= TYPE
Assume binary files are of the given
.IR TYPE :
.BR binary " (default; report a match with a single line)," " text" ", or"
.BR without\-match .
.SS Other options
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-null\-data
Treat input and output as sequences of lines terminated by a NUL byte instead
of a newline.
.TP
.BR \-\-color [ =\fIWHEN\fR ], \-\-colour [ =\fIWHEN\fR ]
Surround matched text with an ANSI color escape.
.I WHEN
is
.BR auto ", " always ", or " never .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
One or more lines were selected.
.TP
.B 1
No lines were selected.
.TP
.B 2
An error occurred (an invalid option, an invalid pattern, or an unreadable
file).  Under
.BR \-q ,
a successful match always yields status 0 even if an error also occurred.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find every occurrence of
.I foo
with file name and line number across a tree:
.PP
.RS
.B grep \-rn foo .
.RE
.PP
Print just the numbers found in a file:
.PP
.RS
.B grep \-oE '[[:digit:]]+' file
.RE
.PP
Use
.B grep
as a test in a script:
.PP
.RS
.B if grep \-q PATTERN file; then ...; fi
.RE
.SH CONFORMING TO
IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (POSIX.1-2024).  The GNU and BSD extensions documented
above are also supported; where the two disagree the BSD (FreeBSD) behavior is
used.  Back-references in basic regular expressions
.RB ( \e1 .. \e9 )
are supported; in extended regular expressions
.B \eN
is a literal digit, matching BSD.  Locale-specific collating symbols
.RB ( [[.ch.]] )
and equivalence classes
.RB ( [[=a=]] )
are not supported (Substrate uses the C/POSIX locale).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sed (1),
.BR regex (3),
.BR fnmatch (3)
